png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨4(\\sin(t)-\\sin(2t)),\\,3(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t))⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3bed103-88e7-5529-bf46-ac64ed093a94" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522984 #edb/ref 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4(\\cos(t)-2\\cos(2t)),\\,3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}4(-\\sin(t)+\\sin(2t))\\cdot 3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}12(\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))(2\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523014 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133896.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523014 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "c4e18406-dc62-5d93-a5b4-7de04271fe9b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540297 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540277 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=7\\cos t\\,i+3\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/88/88907a8e0d33f889ae4fe3723a239b904a302f41fa294fc372e9d73cb049128a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "001b4812-0777-5cfd-acd4-edb5e3c4b5a2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540277 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -7\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}3\\sin t\\cdot(-7\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{21}{2}\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{21}{2}t-\\frac{21}{4}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(21 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=21 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=21 \\pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540316 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=5\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/07/078f9509a08715c2a842bc9b8e12bfb3da9b9d77950275174ac684b399d06a68.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a32b1b6-1443-5fe5-830b-fde1a97630b5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -5\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-5\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(15-15\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[15t-\\frac{15}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(30 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=30 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=30 \\pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540309 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/07/078757ceefd30bb15326569c8fdd2f58b92a1a1cf9679c0d1ea662f387cbe553.png)\n\nConsider the region $